[Music]  this man is on a mission to protect sea  life that is under threat his  discoveries have shocked the scientific  world by pioneering the latest  technology he hopes to save some of the  rarest creatures on the planet  like the giant sea bass a monster of the  deep that can grow to nearly 3 meters  long and swallow a medium-sized shark in  one gulp they're the closest thing you  can get to being extinct with only 500  giant sea bass left in the ocean could a  Facebook for fish help bring them back  from the brink of extinction technology  is both the genesis of the problem and I  think an important part of this solution  [Music]  Douglas McAuley is a professor of marine  sciences at the University of California  Santa Barbara he's on his way to the  Channel Islands of the coast of  California  this unique and delicate ecosystem was  made into a network of marine protected  areas parts of the ocean where fishing  is restricted or banned many scientists  believe that creating more of these  areas could be key to saving some of the  thousands of marine species currently at  risk of disappearing forever the giant  sea bass used to thrive along the  pacific coast but sport fishing and gill  net fishing have decimated the  population last year ninety seven of  them were sold in California alone  that's one-fifth of the current  population all around the world advances  in technology are allowing fishermen to  go further and deeper than ever before  we're in a pretty serious point now for  the future oceans well what I see is an  opportunity in the midst of all this bad  news we have more science that is  telling us how to react to these threats  than we've ever had before  marine-protected areas currently make up  3.7 percent of the ocean but critically  endangered fish like the giant sea bass  don't respect these borders  Doug's work is helping to establish  where else and why fishing should be  banned or restricted while also putting  down a baited underwater camera and  trying to collect some of those images  of giant sea bass if they're in the area  of course they are rare but just hope  luck is with us today  conventional tagging methods are hard to  use with rare species like the giant sea  bass so to track them Doug has developed  a novel alternative he's building a  photographic database that can map their  locations a kind of Facebook for fish  this is the baited remote underwater  camera it's focused here on our bait  cage we'll get this guy set up and ready  to go  [Music]  these cameras are carefully lowered into  the depths where they'll be constantly  recording in a bid to capture a glimpse  of one of these enormous and elusive sea  creatures it's a magical experience when  you look down beneath your fins and  there is wildness you have these  hundred-foot fronds they'll grow from  the bottom to the surface leaking  through the canopy of this kelp forest a  rays of sunlight it really feels like an  underwater Cathedral every time I go in  there I'm blown away that's what a  marine protected area is about about  creating a legacy so that that  inspiration is there for generations to  come  Doug's work tracking the movement of  marine species has already had a major  impact one of his research projects  informed the US government's decision to  expand America's largest marine  protected area we used tags like this  and a study of a shark species called a  grey reef shark which as the name  suggests people always thought lived on  coral reefs put this tag on lo and  behold this shark actually was traveling  way out into the open ocean they were  feeding out in these blue plastic sounds  and the protected areas that we'd set up  for them we're too small so it's a  really great example of how a piece of  technology when you strap into a shark  it can tell you how to be smarter at  setting up protected areas after his  success with GPS for sharks  Doug hopes that his Facebook for giant  sea bass could lead to similar results  if he discovers they're populating other  parts of the ocean he can build a case  to create new marine protected areas and  again it's a piece of technology that  could make the difference back in the  lab Doug is reviewing footage from  recent dives here coming out of the  corner another giant sea bass coming in  so close he can actually see the  parasites in its head interestingly this  guy's got some scars on his pectoral fin  at a fishing line hanging  of him that's a guy who has a hook there  that it will be large dense JA helping  to analyze these images is an algorithm  originally developed by NASA to identify  stars but Doug and his team are now  using it to identify fish on both sides  of a giant sea bass there are spots and  those spots are unique accidentally  these giant sea baths are carrying their  own barcode on the sides of their bodies  the algorithm can quickly scan an  identified individual fish so far Doug  has catalogued 115 of this critically  endangered species and is mapping their  migration patterns we know next to  nothing about giant sea bass a project  like this gives us the raw data to  actually manage their future successful  in marine scientists are increasingly  turning to technology and data to help  reverse the damage being done to our  ocean and to endangered species like the  giant sea bass we need to see more  people like me that are fish nerds  hanging out with computer nerds from  Silicon Valley to understand more about  the oceans to see more on the oceans and  to manage them more effectively I really  see technology as the keystone for being  able to figure out how to get to that  hundred year future that thousand year  future which is healthy for our oceans  [Music]  [Music]  you  [Music]  you

